{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Of [D]all the money that [G]e'er I [D]had,
I [Bm]spent it [G]in good [A]company,
And [D]all the harm that [G]e'er I've [D]done,
A[Bm]las, it [G]was to [A]none but me.
And [D]all I've done for [A]want of [Bm]wit
To [G]memory now I [D]can't re[A]call;
So [D]fill to me the [G]parting [D]glass,
Good [Bm]night and [G]joy be [A]with you [D]all.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
If [D]I had money e[G]nough to [D]spend,
And [Bm]leisure [G]time to [A]sit awhile,
There [D]is a fair maid [G]in this [D]town
That [Bm]sorely [G]has my [A]heart beguiled.
Her [D]rosy cheeks and [A]ruby [Bm]lips,
I [G]own she has my [D]heart en[A]thralled;
So [D]fill to me the [G]parting [D]glass,
Good [Bm]night and [G]joy be [A]with you [D]all.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
Of [D]all the comrades [G]e'er I [D]had,
They're [Bm]sorry [G]for my [A]going away,
And [D]all the sweethearts [G]e'er I [D]had,
They'd [Bm]wish me [G]one more [A]day to stay.
But [D]since it falls un[A]to my [Bm]lot
That [G]I should rise and [D]you should [A]not,
I'll [D]gently rise and [G]softly [D]call,
Good [Bm]night and [G]joy be [A]with you [D]all.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: D}
